<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Chitral State</h1><p class="st-meta">1570 CE – 1969 CE (approx.) · capital Chitral</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountain kingdom of the Hindu Kush ruled by its Mehtars, scene of the famous siege of 1895 and part of Pakistan until 1969.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katur-dynasty">Katur dynasty</a> took power in Chitral in the later 16th century, ruling the high valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunar-river">Kunar</a> (Chitral) river beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirich-mir">Tirich Mir</a>. The rulers took the title Mehtar and contended with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-badakhshan">Badakhshan</a>, the Kushwaqt rulers of upper Chitral and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dards">Dards</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mehtar ruled through a hierarchy of nobles over a population of farmers and herders speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khowar-language">Khowar</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> Muslim communities. Aman ul-Mulk, who died in 1892, united upper and lower Chitral. The succession war after his death led to the siege of the British garrison in Chitral fort in 1895, its relief, and closer British control.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chitral acceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947 under Muzaffar ul-Mulk. Its princely status was abolished in 1969, when it became a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">North-West Frontier Province</a>, now Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chitral-state.jpg" alt="Chitral State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_State_of_Chitral.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1570 CE – 1969 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chitral</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aman ul-Mulk, Shuja ul-Mulk, Muzaffar ul-Mulk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni and Ismaili Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khowar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chitral</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1770&amp;focus=chitral-state" aria-label="Chitral State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6491 883 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chitral State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6811 1139L6905 1198M6811 1139L7364 1602M6811 1139L6778 1519"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6905 1198h0M7364 1602h0M6778 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6811 1139h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6811 1139h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chitral. <a href="/atlas?year=1770&amp;focus=chitral-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

